Title: Student Data Base System
You are developing a student result management system. The database should support updating
records, adding new entries, searching for specific students, and sorting based on performance.
Using an array of structures, implement a student database with attributes: roll no, name, program,
course, subject marks, total, and average. Support operations: dis- play, search, and sort. (Students
can additionally perform modify, append.)
Software Used: ‘C’ programming tool (DEV)
Theory:
Structure is the collection of variables of different types under a single name for better handling.
For example: You want to store the information about person about his/her name, citizenship
number and salary. You can create these information separately but, better approach will be
collection of these information under single name because all these information are related to
person.
Definition and Syntax:
struct structure_name
{
type1 Member1;
type2 Member2;
typeX Memberx;
} ;

Structure variabledeclaration
When a structure is defined, it creates a user-defined type but, no storage is allocated. The
variable can be declared as:
Struct structure_name variable1,variable2;
Accessing members of a structure
There are two types of operators used for accessing members of a structure.
1. Member operator(.)
2. Structure pointer operator(->)
Any member of a structure can be accessed as: structure_variable_name.member_name



Array of Structures:
Arrays are one of the most widely used data structures in programming languages. One
downfall of using such a data type is that one must use homogeneous data types, an array can
only hold multiple items of the same type. Structures overcome this problem by allowing the
programmer to have an unlimited number of items of different data types! Objects contained
within the structure are referred to as members.
Syntax:
struct structure_name
{
type1 Member1;
type2 Member2;
typeXMemberx;
} listname[X];
Here we can declare copy name as our instance name and disregard the name field. We can use
list name[x] if we would we know that we will be immediately needing x copies of ourstructure.
Algorithm:
Step 1: Start
Step 2: Define a structure Student with fields:
roll (Roll No)
name
program
course
marks[5]
total
avg
Step 3: Declare:
An array db[100] of students.
Integer n = 0 to store the number of students.
Step 4: Display Menu repeatedly until the user chooses Exit:
a. Add Student
Read roll no, name, program, and course.
Read 5 subject marks.
Compute total = sum of marks.
Compute average = total / 5.
Store the record in db[n] and increment n.
b. Display Students
Print headings (Roll, Name, Program, Course, Total, Average).
Loop through all records i = 0 to n-1 and print their details.


c. Search Student
Input roll number to search.
Loop through all records:
If db[i].roll matches, print student details.
If no match found, display "Student Not Found".
d. Sort Students (by performance)
Use nested loops to compare total marks of each student.
If db[i].total < db[j].total, swap records.
Display "Students sorted by performance".
e. Modify Student Record
Input roll number to modify.
Search for matching roll number.
If found:
Update name, program, course.
Input new 5 subject marks.
Recalculate total and average.
Display "Record modified".
If not found: display "Record not found".
f. Exit
Terminate the program.
Step 5: Stop
Conclusion:



[[[[[[[   PROGRAM    ]]]]]


  
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
struct Student {
int roll;
char name[50];
char program[20];
char course[20];
int marks[5];
int total;
float avg;
};
struct Student db[100];
int n = 0; // number of students
void addStudent() {
int i;
printf("\nEnter Roll No: ");
scanf("%d", &db[n].roll);
printf("Enter Name: ");
scanf("%s", db[n].name);
printf("Enter Program: ");
scanf("%s", db[n].program);
printf("Enter Course: ");
scanf("%s", db[n].course);
db[n].total = 0;
printf("Enter 5 subject marks: ");
for(i=0; i<5; i++) {
scanf("%d", &db[n].marks[i]);
db[n].total += db[n].marks[i];
}
db[n].avg = db[n].total / 5.0;
n++;
printf("Record added successfully!\n");
}
void displayStudents() {
int i,j;
printf("\n%-5s %-10s %-10s %-10s %-10s %-10s\n", "Roll", "Name", "Program", "Course",
"Total", "Average");
for(i=0; i<n; i++) {
printf("%-5d %-10s %-10s %-10s %-10d %-10.2f\n",
db[i].roll, db[i].name, db[i].program, db[i].course, db[i].total, db[i].avg);
}
}
void searchStudent() {
int roll, i, found=0;
printf("\nEnter Roll No to search: ");
scanf("%d", &roll);
for(i=0; i<n; i++) {
if(db[i].roll == roll) {
printf("Record Found: %s (%d) Total: %d Avg: %.2f\n",
db[i].name, db[i].roll, db[i].total, db[i].avg);
found=1;
break;
}
}
if(!found) printf("Student Not Found!\n");
}
void sortStudents() {
int i, j;
struct Student temp;
for(i=0; i<n-1; i++) {
for(j=i+1; j<n; j++) {
if(db[i].total < db[j].total) {
temp = db[i];
db[i] = db[j];
db[j] = temp;
}
}
}
printf("Students sorted by performance (total marks).\n");
}
void modifyStudent() {
int roll, i, found=0;
printf("\nEnter Roll No to modify: ");
scanf("%d", &roll);
for(i=0; i<n; i++) {
if(db[i].roll == roll) {
printf("Enter new Name: ");
scanf("%s", db[i].name);
printf("Enter new Program: ");
scanf("%s", db[i].program);
printf("Enter new Course: ");
scanf("%s", db[i].course);
db[i].total = 0;
printf("Enter 5 subject marks: ");
for(int j=0;j<5;j++) {
scanf("%d", &db[i].marks[j]);
db[i].total += db[i].marks[j];
}
db[i].avg = db[i].total/5.0;
printf("Record modified!\n");
found=1;
break;
}
}
if(!found) printf("Record not found!\n");
}
int main() {
int choice;
while(1) {
printf("\n--- Student Database Menu ---\n");
printf("1. Add Student\n2. Display Students\n3. Search Student\n4. Sort Students\n5. Modify
Student\n6. Exit\n");
printf("Enter choice: ");
scanf("%d", &choice);
switch(choice) {
case 1: addStudent(); break;
case 2: displayStudents(); break;
case 3: searchStudent(); break;
case 4: sortStudents(); break;
case 5: modifyStudent(); break;
case 6: return 0;
default: printf("Invalid choice!\n");
}
}
}
